  :adr/title "The CLI leaves the app, and the pinned surface gets a checker that refuses"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-07"
  :adr/related "adr-2609075400-the-shape-of-a-five-repository-split"
  :adr/body "## What was asked

cloud-itonami-app / -cli / -api / -apex に整理してほしい、-auth も分離。

This ADR lands the first of those four. Nothing is deleted from the app yet.

## The seam, measured twice

My first measurement (adr-2609075400) said `-cli` was clean because
`commands.cljc` and `repo_profile.cljc` require nothing from the app. That is
true and it is the wrong half of the question. What decides whether they can
move is who requires THEM, and I had not looked.

Looking:

    cloud.itonami.app.repo-profile   required by: bin/itonami, its own test
    cloud.itonami.app.commands       required by: bin/itonami, tests, cli.clj

So `commands` had one app-side consumer, and that consumer decides the
dependency direction between two of the four repositories. If `cli.clj` is a
live front end, moving `commands` makes the app depend on the CLI, which is
backwards; leaving it makes the CLI depend on an app checkout at runtime,
which is the coupling the split exists to end.

`cli.clj` is neither. There is no `:cli` alias in `deps.edn` -- the file's own
comment at line 591 says a test goes red \"the day :cli is reintroduced\". It is
tested but has no launcher. PR #270 removed `:server`, `:mcp` and `:cli` and
left `bin/itonami` refusing every command; `bin/itonami` is what replaced it.

So the CLI's namespaces have no live app-side consumer, and they move as a
unit. The question that looked like a design fork was a measurement I had not
taken.

## What moved

    bin/itonami + 6 modules   editor, harness, profile, skills, splash, text
    commands.cljc             what a command is
    repo_profile.cljc         which bot a repository selects
    5 nbb suites              editor 63/206, harness 16/41, skills 11/36,
                              splash 17/55, client 9 checks
    3 resources               pinned, see below

Proven standalone before anything else: `./bin/itonami --print-data-dir`
resolves to the CLI repository, and `./bin/itonami status` answers the live
resident (409 routes, 209 commands, store 5a6c4a52). It reads no file from the
app tree.

## The three pinned files, and the gate they need

`commands.edn` is derived from the app's route table by the app's `route-scan`
and checked by the app's own test. `cli-aliases.edn` and `defaults.edn` are
the app's too. The CLI pins copies so it runs without an app checkout beside
it -- and a pinned copy of a generated file is exactly the thing that drifts.

`scripts/verify-pinned-surface.cljs` answers with three outcomes: 0 identical,
1 drift (naming the file and both digests), 2 REFUSED when no app checkout was
found. The third is the point -- nothing was compared, so it is not a pass.

The first version of that function did not discriminate. It built a candidate
list and took the first path that existed, so `--app /nonexistent` fell
through to the sibling default and returned exit 0: a wrong path reported as a
match. The control written to prove REFUSED works is what caught it. An
explicitly named directory is now authoritative; only the implicit sibling is
allowed to be absent.

Controls, all run: clean 0 · perturbed alias file 1, named · `--app
/nonexistent` 2 · `CLOUD_ITONAMI_APP_DIR=/nonexistent` 2 · clean again 0.

## What has NOT happened

- Nothing is deleted from `cloud-itonami-app`. Both trees carry the CLI, both
  suites pass, and `~/.local/bin/itonami` still resolves through
  `~/.cloud-itonami/current`. Removing the app's copy means repointing that
  launcher, and it is a separate change so there is never a moment when
  nothing works.
- `-api`, `-apex` and `-auth` are not started. `-apex` is next and is
  separable today (five Workers with their own wrangler configs). `-auth`
  needs a decision about what the app asks it FOR, and `-api` needs
  `server.clj` split into routes and handlers first -- a refactor inside the
  current repo that does not need a repository to begin.

## Why a repository at all

The honest answer is not \"the app is too big\". It is that these two things
have different release cadences and different reasons to change: the client
changes when the terminal experience changes, and the server changes when the
routes change. The registry is the contract between them, and until today that
contract was implicit in a shared directory. Now it is three files with a
digest and a checker that refuses to guess."}]
